onfidence":0.98},"academicOrientation":{"score":34,"confidence":0.9},"profileConfidence":0.96,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"0aca48a13576b14f4f174e919966f70353e54c3f794338752d44f1cf7dc2f9bf","reviewStatus":"flagged","validationWarnings":["central_figures_hit_schema_cap"]},"primarySubject":"Politics & Government","secondarySubjects":["American History","Journalism & Reportage"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores the dynamics of presidential power within the context of American government while incorporating elements of political history and investigative journalism.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"efd92a9d8251ed8f829730b9196bb4907bd139c0e683ccc05871173e09553e92","summary":"In 1789, the Founding Fathers came up with a system of checks and balances to keep kingly powers out of the hands of American presidents. But in the 1970s and '80s, a faction of Republican loyalists, outraged by the fall of the imperial presidency after Watergate and the Vietnam War, abandoned conservatives' traditional suspicion of concentrated government power. These men hatched a plot that would allow the White House to return to, or even surpass, the virtually unchecked powers that Richard Nixon had briefly tried to wield. Congress would be defanged, and the commander-in-chief would be able to assert a unilateral dominance both at home and abroad. Today, this plot is coming to fruition. As Takeover reveals, the Bush-Cheney administration has succeeded in seizing vast powers for the presidency by throwing off many of the restraints placed upon it by Congress, the courts, and the Constitution. This timely book unveils the secret machinations behind the headlines, explaining the links between warrantless wiretapping and the President Bush's Supreme Court nominees, between the torture debate and the secrecy surrounding Vice President Cheney's energy task force, and between the \"faith-based initiative\" and the holding of US citizens without trial as \"enemy combatants.\"","publicationYear":2007,"displaySummary":"In 1789, the Founding Fathers came up with a system of checks and balances to keep kingly powers out of the hands of American presidents. But in the 1970s and '80s, a faction of Republican loyalists, outraged by the fall of the imperial presidency after Watergate and the Vietnam War, abandoned conservatives' traditional suspicion of concentrated government power. These men hatched a plot that would allow the White House to return to, or even surpass, the virtually unchecked powers that Richard Nixon had briefly tried to wield. Congress would be defanged, and the commander-in-chief would be able to assert a unilateral dominance both at home and abroad. Today, this plot is coming to fruition. As Takeover reveals, the Bush-Cheney administration has succeeded in seizing vast powers for the presidency by throwing off many of the restraints placed upon it by Congress, the courts, and the Constitution. This timely book unveils the secret machinations behind the headlines, explaining the links between warrantless wiretapping and the President Bush's Supreme Court nominees, between the torture debate and the secrecy surrounding Vice President Cheney's energy task force, and between the \"faith-based initiative\" and the holding of US citizens without trial as \"enemy combatants.\"","primaryTopic":"Vietnam War","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Politics & Government","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Politics & Government","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Politics & Government","mappedSubject":"Politics & Government","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily explores the dynamics of presidential power within the context of American government while incorporating elements of political history and investigative journalism.","id":"subject-evidence-book-takeover-the-